Almost completely agreed, except for the conclusion: I think it should
be discouraged. Identifying items is, in my opinion, what NetHack is all
about, and price-IDing makes it much too easy.

> I'm not sure if it this is really such a major feat. Without spoilers
> the building of the shopkeeper buying prices is one easiest things to
> do. After one realizes that they always buy specific items for the
> same price. The selling prices are much harder.

It's only easy if you spend lots of games on it. If you're a new player,
how could you ever determine that a scroll of identify is 10 zm? You'd
have to resort to tying out scrolls eventually, slowly building up your
pricelist, and also have to play lots of games in succession to notice
that the scroll of identify (and other items) are priced the same every
game.

> As it is now, it is quite easy for the experienced player. But I can't
> think of a way to make it harder without also making it harder for an
> unspoiled player.

No issue for me: I want to make it more difficult for *everyone*.

> As a spoiled one, I would find it more challenging if one or two items
> from the same price group could "drop" to the lower group for the
> duration of a whole game. Right now, this can only happen with few
> items for example scrolls of enchant armour or remove curse which can
> be confused with enchant weapon.

Very easy to circumvent this problem. Just drop the items to get the
price, and it's a possible "collision of prices", just leave the shop,
go back in, and try again. The chance of prices being one third off is
less than 50%, so if you try the ducious scrolls a few times this way,
you are almost sure what they really are. It just takes a few turns
more.

